-- title:
--   Adjoining a p-th root of an S-unit, p ∈ S
-- statement:
--   Fix a finite set $S$ of rational primes and a prime $p \in S$. Let $F$ be an intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (here $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`) which is unramified outside $S$, meaning: $F$ is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb{Q}$, and for every prime $q \notin S$ and every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $q$ (that is, $q$ is a non-unit of $A$), the image in $\mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb{Q}}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ of the inertia subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, transported along the inclusion of the decomposition subgroup, is contained in the subgroup fixing $F$ pointwise. Let $x \in F$ be such that for every prime $q \notin S$ and every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $q$, both $x$ and $x^{-1}$ belong to $A$ (so $x$ is a unit at every such $A$). Let $\alpha \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ satisfy $\alpha^{p} = x$. Then the compositum $F \sqcup \mathbb{Q}(\alpha)$, the join of $F$ with the intermediate field generated by $\alpha$, is again unramified outside $S$ in the same sense: it is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb{Q}$ and, for every $q \notin S$ and every valuation subring over $q$, the inertia subgroup fixes it pointwise.
--
--   This is the Kummer-theoretic step that extensions obtained by adjoining $p$-th roots of $S$-units are unramified outside $S$, provided $p \in S$. It is used in the arithmetic of $S$-ramified extensions of number fields, notably to pass to a field over which $S$-units become $p$-divisible, in the computations with $H^1$ of $S$-unit and $S$-class-group representations that cite it.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IntermediateField_isUnramifiedOutside_sup_adjoin_of_pow_eq.lean

